==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fr|en}} {{term|galvaniser|lang=fr}}, from {{term|galvanisme|lang=fr}}, after [[physiologist]] [[w:Luigi Galvani|Luigi Alyisio Galvani]] (1737&ndash;1798).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈɡælvənaɪ̯z/|lang=en}}

===Alternative forms===
* [[galvanise]] (''mostly UK'')

===Verb===
{{en-verb|galvaniz}}

# {{context|transitive|chiefly|_|North America|chemistry|lang=en}} To coat with a thin layer of [[metal]] by [[electrochemical]] means; to [[electroplate]].
# {{context|transitive|chiefly|_|North America|lang=en}} To coat with rust-resistant [[zinc]]
# {{context|transitive|chiefly|_|North America|lang=en}} To [[shock]] or [[stimulate]] into sudden activity
# {{context|transitive|archaic|lang=en}} To [[electrify]].
#* {{w|Thomas Babington Macaulay}}
#*: The agitations resembled the grinnings and writhings of a '''galvanized''' corpse, not the struggles of an athletic man.

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|to shock or stimulate}} [[startle]]

